#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130207} :activity/id #uuid "82aa6e24-e2d2-572b-b1aa-bfcb7c8c2948" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050343 :topic/id #uuid "412fa5f9-c876-59a3-911a-6d7386c4f3f7" :topic/math-academy-id 2059} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130456 :tutorial/content "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6d9a5c4-532b-53e1-9689-7b5dbc30ec98" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8d1c0646-172c-5530-a344-03c704adfee5" :step/math-academy-id 42893 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130208}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129267 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128844 :question/id #uuid "c6495ab5-3756-50e6-8977-7ec19fba4466" :question/math-academy-id "e-11470"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3688ce9-ceff-524b-ac44-0224526c861b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049694}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427902}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Cylindrical Solid"} :step/id #uuid "4052d2ac-00ee-5541-8083-57bb342065db" :step/math-academy-id 42894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128845 :question/id #uuid "043cc93d-1eae-5640-a109-9acbe7a34f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-11471"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aea71b56-dd09-564e-a2bf-5606233e2819" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537081} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537072}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid"} :step/id #uuid "04baaba8-4f7b-509d-81e6-12d1b9edece3" :step/math-academy-id 42895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128856 :question/id #uuid "a1b2e13a-95cd-5e0f-aa97-c3e90433560c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12402"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ff4534b-6798-5f33-9823-643705229080" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542431} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542430}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Cylindrical Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "b4729478-340f-5423-b9c8-e2511cd7d6c2" :step/math-academy-id 44539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470 :tutorial/content "Let's now build some intuition behind the meaning of the volume element $\\textrm d V.$ This will help us to understand the change of variables formula in cylindrical polar coordinates. We start by considering the usual rectangular coordinate system.\n\nSuppose we have a point $P$ and a neighboring point $Q$ in the Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below. \n\n![](images/25/2535744585beb7fed89d760c0290a04f54d0a08c8f7e564b17c8e42432da031e.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d x$ parallel to the $x$-axis so that the new point $P'$ has the same $x$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we move a distance $\\textrm d y$ parallel to the $y$-axis so that the new point $P''$ has the same $y$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ parallel to the $z$-axis to land to $Q.$\n\nThese movements span the rectangular solid shown above. The volume of this solid, denoted $\\textrm d V,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}z.\n$$\n\nThus, the element $\\textrm d V$ represents the volume generated by moving from the point $P$ to the point $Q$ in this coordinate system." :tutorial/id #uuid "82dc2175-bfda-5b86-bba2-acb36d6cd7ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7731 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Volume Element in Cartesian Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "c603c442-b1a4-5adf-ae0d-730bdeda4732" :step/math-academy-id 44790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130473 :tutorial/content "Let's now repeat this process for a cylindrical polar system. Consider the points $P$ and $Q$ shown below.\n\n![](images/24/243d720546357a33eac3f96b84755cf6aeebafdef3a3672a3d29989147f134c2.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d r$ so that the new point $P'$ has the same $r$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we sweep through an angle $\\textrm d \\theta$ so that the new point $P''$ has the same $\\theta$-coordinate as $Q.$ The arc length traced out by this movement approximately equals $r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ vertically to land on $Q.$\n\nThese three movements trace out a solid that's approximately rectangular, and so the approximate volume of this solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}r \\cdot r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\mathrm{d}z=  r\\,\\mathrm{d}z\\,\\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a132073-0472-5027-b85c-8d1bed6673d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7772 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Volume Element in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "e1410a08-b410-5156-bc97-92666f25c17f" :step/math-academy-id 45005}] :activity/title "Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129389}]}
